a strict mode in JavaScript tightens the  rules for certain behaviors these  restrictions keep code safer and  sometimes help JavaScript engines  perform optimizations you can execute  JavaScript code in strict mode by using  the use strict directive there are two  ways to use strict mode you can apply it  to your entire file by putting you  strict as the first line in your file  here's an example of that even before  this comment I'm going to put use strict  and now this entire file will be run in  strict mode you can also apply strict  mode to individual functions by putting  use strict at the first line in a  function here's an example of that see  we have you strict as the first line of  the function and then we have all the  other lines of the function strict mode  is going to convert mistakes or bad  syntax into real errors in normal  JavaScript mistyping a variable name  creates a new global variable however in  strict mode this will throw an error  making it impossible to accidentally  create a global variable if you look up  here at this example this is going to  create an error in strict mode in strict  mode you have to first declare a  variable with var Const or let before  you can assign a value to it so I could  put bar y equals 3.14 and now this will  not create an error also in strict mode  deleting a variable is not allowed it is  allowed in normal mode in normal  JavaScript a developer will not receive  any error feedback assigning values to  non writable properties in strict mode  in the assignment to a non writable  property a getter only property a  non-existing property a non-existing  variable or a non-existing object will  throw an error so this is an example of  trying to write to a non writable  property we've created this property X  and we've set writable to false so you  should not be able to change that value  to 3.14 and in strict mode that will  create an error now here's an example of  trying to write  to a Gitter only property so this is a  get property so you should only be able  to get it you shouldn't be able to  assign a new value to it  so in strict mode that will throw an  error also strict mode makes attempting  to delete undeletable properties throw  an error where before the attempt would  simply just have no effect you cannot  delete object at prototype that's going  to throw an error in a normal mode it  would not delete just would have no  effect in strict mode function parameter  names must be unique so if you have a  function like this and we use strict in  the function or even if the whole file  is using strict you can't have two A's  as parameters this is going to be wrong  because it's probably just a mistake  right here you're trying to return a  plus B plus C but we have a a C so  that's just gonna give a syntax error  there are also changes to width and eval  in strict mode the width statement  extends the scope chain for a statement  strict mode prohibits with completely  the problem with width is that any name  inside the block might map either to a  property or the object passed to it or  to a variable n surrounding or even  global scope at runtime it's impossible  to know beforehand so when we use width  here that means we're going to add the  scope to whatever we passed in as a  parameter to all the code within the  width block if this were not strict mode  would this X refer to var X or would it  refer to objects because we passed in  it's actually impossible to say without  running the code so the name can't be  optimized so prohibiting the use of  width makes optimization better the  other changes with eval in strict mode  eval does not introduce new variables  into the surrounding scope so in normal  code if you have eval so if you have  eval var X that's going to introduce a  variable X into the surrounding function  or the global scope in strict  eval creates variables only for the code  being evaluated so eval can't affect  whether a name refers to an outer  variable or some local variable this VAR  X is only going to exist right in this  little section of code here so outside  of that there will not be any of our X  so here's another example of that we're  gonna set X to 17 and for about X we are  going to eval all the code from within  here so we're gonna evaluate that code  and you can see it's it's strictmode VAR  x equals 42 and x we're gonna pass back  X to eval X so in normal mode putting  var x equals 42 within this eval  statement is going to change the X we  put up here to 17 however in strict mode  X is still going to equal 17 and eval X  is now gonna equal 42 because this X  that we're passing back to eval X is  completely different than this X also  strict mode makes it easier to write  secure JavaScript in browsers it's no  longer possible to reference the window  object through this inside of strict  mode function and strict mode makes it  impossible to easily access the most  recently called function and the  arguments used to run a function these  features of strict mode have a lot of  security advantages because of the  security advantages and because it helps  with ITER's a lot of people recommend  that you always use strict mode on your  code well those are the basics of strict  mode thanks for watching my name is beau  Carnes don't forget to subscribe and  remember use your code for good
 

